        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 事故",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "事故 — shìgù — \"accident\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "这个路口常常发生事故 zhège lùkǒu chángcháng fāshēng shìgù — Accidents often happen at this crossing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 故 (gù) — nine strokes in two pieces, the right two pieces of 做 without its person piece: on the left 十 over 口, five strokes; on the right the striking piece, four strokes. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "事故 (shìgù) is built from 事 'a matter' and 故 (gù), both inherited from Middle Chinese, dzriH and kuH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the second 'old' and 'a matter'; how the pair came to mean an accident is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
